---
import Container from "@/components/container.astro";
import Sectionhead from "@/components/sectionhead.astro";
---

<Container>
  <Sectionhead>
    <Fragment slot="title">Development Overview</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="desc">
      A deeper look into how CerebraSense was built.
    </Fragment>
  </Sectionhead>

  <article class="development-content mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl">
    <section>
      <p>
        The development of CerebraSense was grounded in a practical research
        objective: to build a lightweight deep learning system capable of
        classifying brain MRI scans into tumor-related categories. The system
        was designed around accuracy, controlled experimentation, and future
        deployment readiness.
      </p>
      <p>
        The notebook began with a structured comparison of manually implemented
        neural network scenarios using NumPy. These experiments tested how
        architecture depth, hidden-layer width, regularization, optimizer
        choice, activation function, and class-sensitive training affected MRI
        classification performance. After the dense-network experiments, the
        development moved toward a custom convolutional neural network because
        MRI images contain spatial patterns that are better captured through
        convolutional feature extraction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The final selected model is a custom CNN trained from scratch. It does
        not rely on a pre-trained architecture. Instead, the model uses three
        convolutional blocks, a compact fully connected classifier, label
        smoothing, early stopping, and learning-rate scheduling to balance
        performance and generalization.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Dataset & Preprocessing</h2>
      <p>
        The notebook uses the Brain Tumor MRI Dataset by Masoud Nickparvar from
        Kaggle. The dataset supports multi-class brain MRI classification and
        is organized into four classes: glioma, meningioma, no tumor, and
        pituitary tumor.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the current implementation, the dataset is stored in separate
        training and testing folders. The training folder is converted into
        <code>brain_tumor_train.csv</code>, while the testing folder is
        converted into <code>brain_tumor_test.csv</code>. Each image is
        processed with the same conversion pipeline before model training.
      </p>
      <p>During preprocessing, each MRI image is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Loaded from its class folder.</li>
        <li>Converted to grayscale using a single intensity channel.</li>
        <li>Resized to 32 x 32 pixels.</li>
        <li>Flattened into 1,024 pixel features.</li>
        <li>Stored in CSV format with the class label as the first column.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The class label mapping used in the notebook is:</p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th class="numeric">Label</th>
              <th>Class</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">0</td>
              <td>Glioma</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">1</td>
              <td>Meningioma</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">2</td>
              <td>No Tumor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">3</td>
              <td>Pituitary</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        For the final CNN, the flattened pixel values are standardized using
        <code>StandardScaler</code>. The scaled vectors are then reshaped back
        into image tensors with shape <code>1 x 32 x 32</code> so that the CNN
        can learn spatial features from the MRI scans.
      </p>

      <h3>Dataset Class Distribution</h3>
