      <p>
        The class distribution is relatively balanced. The no tumor class has
        the largest number of training samples, while the glioma class has the
        smallest. Since the imbalance ratio is only 1.21, the dataset is
        suitable for baseline experimentation without severe imbalance
        correction. Earlier notebook scenarios explored class weighting, but
        the final CNN relies mainly on stratified splitting, label smoothing,
        dropout, and weight decay for generalization.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Model Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        The final selected model is a custom convolutional neural network for
        four-class brain MRI classification. The input layer receives a
        grayscale MRI image reshaped as a <code>1 x 32 x 32</code> tensor.
      </p>
      <p>The CNN contains three convolutional blocks:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Convolution Block 1: <code>Conv2d(1, 32, kernel_size=3, padding=1)</code>,
          ReLU activation, and 2 x 2 max pooling.
        </li>
        <li>
          Convolution Block 2: <code>Conv2d(32, 64, kernel_size=3, padding=1)</code>,
          ReLU activation, and 2 x 2 max pooling.
        </li>
        <li>
          Convolution Block 3: <code>Conv2d(64, 128, kernel_size=3, padding=1)</code>,
          ReLU activation, and 2 x 2 max pooling.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        After the convolutional blocks, the feature maps are flattened and
        passed through a compact classifier:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Fully Connected Layer: 2,048 input features to 128 neurons with ReLU
          activation.
        </li>
        <li>Dropout Layer: dropout rate of 0.3.</li>
        <li>
          Output Layer: 4 logits corresponding to glioma, meningioma, no tumor,
          and pituitary.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The model outputs raw logits during training. Softmax is applied during
        evaluation and inference to convert logits into class probabilities.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Training & Optimization</h2>
      <p>
        Several strategies were used to improve learning stability and reduce
        overfitting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The dataset was standardized using <code>StandardScaler</code>, fitted
        only on the training data and reused for validation, testing, and
        inference. A stratified 80/20 split was applied to the training CSV to
        create the training and validation subsets.
      </p>
      <p>The final CNN was trained using PyTorch with the following configuration:</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Setting</th>
              <th>Value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Optimizer</td>
              <td>Adam</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Learning Rate</td>
              <td>0.001</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Weight Decay</td>
              <td>1e-4</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Loss Function</td>
              <td>Cross-entropy with label smoothing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Label Smoothing</td>
              <td>0.1</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Batch Size</td>
              <td>64</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Maximum Epochs</td>
              <td>50</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Early Stopping Patience</td>
              <td>6 epochs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scheduler</td>
              <td>ReduceLROnPlateau</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scheduler Factor</td>
              <td>0.5</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scheduler Patience</td>
              <td>2 epochs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Random Seed</td>
              <td>42</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Label smoothing was added to reduce overconfident predictions,
        especially for visually similar tumor classes. Adam handled adaptive
        gradient updates, while weight decay discouraged overly large weights.
        Dropout in the dense classifier further reduced overfitting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The learning rate scheduler monitored validation loss and reduced the
        learning rate when improvement slowed. Early stopping halted training
        when validation loss failed to improve for six consecutive epochs. The
        best validation-loss checkpoint was saved as
        <code>best_cnn_label_smoothing1.pt</code> and reloaded before final test
        evaluation.
      </p>
      <p>After evaluation, the deployment-ready artifacts were saved:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>cnn_model1.pt</code> for the trained CNN weights.</li>
        <li><code>scaler.pkl1</code> for the fitted standardization pipeline.</li>
        <li>
          <code>metadata.pkl1</code> for image size, class count, and class
          mapping.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Evaluation Metrics</h2>
      <p>
        The final CNN achieved strong performance on the unseen test set. It
        reached a test accuracy of 97.33%, correctly classifying 1,276 out of
        1,311 MRI images.
      </p>
      <p>
        The best observed validation accuracy during training was 96.33%, while
        the selected best-loss checkpoint evaluated at approximately 97.33% on
        the test set. The small difference between validation and test
        performance suggests that the model generalized well under the current
        dataset split.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strongest class-level performance appeared in the no tumor and
        pituitary classes, both of which reached perfect recall on the test
        set. Most remaining errors occurred between glioma and meningioma,
        which is consistent with the notebook's observation that these classes
        can share similar visual characteristics.
      </p>

