    <section>
      <h2>Future Improvements</h2>
      <p>
        While CerebraSense achieved strong classification performance, several
        improvements can make the system more robust and clinically useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        One important improvement is data augmentation. The final CNN currently
        uses standardized 32 x 32 grayscale images without a full augmentation
        pipeline. Applying controlled rotation, zooming, shifting, horizontal
        flipping, contrast adjustment, and noise injection could expose the
        model to more realistic MRI variation and improve robustness on unseen
        scans.
      </p>
      <p>
        Another direction is testing higher image resolutions. The 32 x 32
        format makes the model lightweight and fast, but aggressive downsampling
        may remove fine tumor boundaries or subtle anatomical features.
        Comparing 32 x 32, 64 x 64, and 128 x 128 inputs would help measure the
        tradeoff between speed and diagnostic detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        The model could also be extended with interpretability tools such as
        Grad-CAM or saliency maps. This would help show which image regions
        influence the CNN's prediction and make the system easier to evaluate
        in a medical imaging context.
      </p>
      <p>
        Transfer learning is also a promising future enhancement. Although the
        current CNN was intentionally trained from scratch, future versions
        could compare it against medical-imaging or general-vision backbones
        such as ResNet. This may improve convergence and robustness, especially
        if the system is trained on larger or more diverse MRI datasets.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, the deployment pipeline should be consolidated. The notebook
        currently saves model weights, scaler, and metadata as separate files. A
        future application version should organize these artifacts into a clear
        inference module, include model versioning, preserve the exact
        preprocessing pipeline, and validate predictions on external MRI
        datasets before any clinical-facing use.
      </p>
    </section>
